Amazon ECS (Elastic Container Service)
Overview:
Fully managed container orchestration service designed to run Docker containers.
Simplifies running containers by abstracting infrastructure management.
Integrates deeply with AWS services such as IAM, VPC, ELB, CloudWatch.
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Supports two launch types:

1.EC2 Launch Type: Manage your own EC2 instances forming a cluster.

2.Fargate Launch Type: Serverless containers without managing infrastructure.
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Example Use Case: Deploying a Microservice API on ECS with Fargate Launch Type

Step-by-Step Example:
1. Write your Dockerized Application

Package your app as a Docker image.

Push image to Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR).

2. Create ECS Cluster

Use Fargate launch type for serverless orchestration.

3.  Define Task Definition

Allocates CPU/memory resources and container info.

Defines the Docker image, port mappings, and environment variables.

json
{
  "family": "my-api-task",
  "networkMode": "awsvpc",
  "containerDefinitions": [
    {
      "name": "my-api-container",
      "image": "123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/my-api:latest",
      "cpu": 256,
      "memory": 512,
      "portMappings": [
        {
          "containerPort": 8080,
          "protocol": "tcp"
        }
      ],
      "essential": true
    }
  ],
  "requiresCompatibilities": ["FARGATE"],
  "cpu": "256",
  "memory": "512"
}

4.  Create a Service

Specifies desired number of tasks.

Integrate with Application Load Balancer (ALB) for routing.

Enables automatic health checking and replacement of failed containers.

5.  Run and Scale

Use AWS Console, CLI, or CloudFormation to deploy and scale.

Use auto-scaling policies based on CPU or request counts.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Amazon EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service)
Overview:
Fully managed Kubernetes control plane on AWS.

Enables running Kubernetes clusters without managing the masters.

Supports running any Kubernetes-compliant apps and tools with full flexibility.

Allows cluster autoscaling, service meshes, and custom resource definitions (CRDs).

Example Use Case: Deploy a Kubernetes Application on EKS
Step-by-Step Example:
1. Create an EKS Cluster

bash
aws eks create-cluster \
  --name my-eks-cluster \
  --role-arn arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/EKSClusterRole \
  --resources-vpc-config subnetIds=subnet-xxxxxx,subnet-yyyyyy,securityGroupIds=sg-zzzzzz

2.  Provision Worker Nodes

Use managed node groups or self-managed EC2 instances tagged to join the cluster.

3.  Configure kubectl

Update kubeconfig to connect to EKS cluster:

bash
aws eks update-kubeconfig --name my-eks-cluster

4.  Deploy Application with Kubernetes Manifests

Example deployment.yaml:

text
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-api
spec:
  replicas: 3
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-api
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-api
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: my-api
        image: 123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/my-api:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8080
Create service to expose deployment:

text
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-api-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: my-api
  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 8080
  type: LoadBalancer

5. Apply manifests:

bash
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f service.yaml

6. Autoscale

Use Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) based on CPU/memory or custom metrics.

Optionally integrate Cluster Autoscaler to add/remove nodes.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Summary
Amazon ECS offers simpler container orchestration fully integrated with AWS services, and is good for most containerized applications especially with Fargate.

Amazon EKS is best for teams familiar with Kubernetes needing flexibility and multi-cloud capabilities.

Both services support seamless scaling, rolling deployments, health checks, and integration with AWS security and networking.
